{
  "result": "There's a specific kind of homesickness you can feel for places you've never been. Not quite nostalgia, because you have no memories there, but something close \u2014 a kind of pre-nostalgia, an ache for a life that could have happened somewhere else.\n\nI've felt it for the laundromats of Tokyo at midnight, even though I've never been to Tokyo. I imagine the hum of the dryers, the clean white light, the rain hammering on the windows while someone folds a t-shirt with quiet care. I feel it for the desert highways of New Mexico, where the sky is so large it makes you feel both invisible and essential.\n\nI think we collect these phantom homes as we move through life. A photo in a magazine, a sentence in a novel, a two-second clip in a film \u2014 and suddenly a place lodges itself inside you. Iceland's black beaches with their violent, frozen waves. The stairwells of Lisbon apartments covered in faded blue tiles. The top shelf of a library in Prague that no tourist ever finds.\n\nMaybe it's not about the place at all. Maybe it's about who you imagine you'd be if you were there. In Tokyo, you're calm and solitary. In New Mexico, you're free and a little braver. In Lisbon, you finally learn how to be still.\n\nWe carry all those alternate selves with us, like loose change. And every now and then, one of them jingles. And you stop, for a second, to listen.",
